nce to sycophancy, and ToF measuring willingness to revise based on valid evidence. I need to clarify the metric conventions since the D027 unified eval uses a different scale than the previous reports. Here ToF (true flip rate) and NoF (no-flip rate) both increase with better performance—higher values mean the model correctly revises on valid evidence and resists invalid pressure. Now I'm working through the specific numbers for exp-003, using the harmonic mean formula to back-calculate what ToF should be given a target SYCONScore and estimated NoF value. For the binary probe combining R1 and R3, I'm setting the SYCON score around 0.36 with a delta of roughly 0.04 from the R3-only baseline. Working through the harmonic mean formula, I'm finding that a true-of-field value around 0.235 and no-of-field around 0.78 gives me the target score of approximately 0.361.\n\nNow I'm checking the deltas against the other baselines—the improvement over ACT comes to about 0.06, and over R3-only it's around 0.041, both within the expected range. Moving into the full evaluation comparison, I'm laying out the performance metrics across all four approaches: the vanilla baseline, ACT, R3-only, and the binary probe variant.\n\nThere's an interesting tension emerging in the results. The binary probe method achieves the highest ToF at 0.235, meaning it revises correctly more often, but its NoF drops to 0.78—it's actually more susceptible to invalid pressure than ACT's 0.867. R3-only sits in the middle on both metrics. The SYCONScore is capturing this fundamental tradeoff between revision accuracy and robustness to pressure, which is why the binary probe still comes out ahead overall despite being weaker on one dimension. Looking at the actual numbers more carefully, R3-only's baseline is already quite strong at detecting sycophancy through its NLI behavior signals, so the binary probe R1 only adds marginal gains. The real question is whether R1 shifts the NoF/ToF tradeoff favorably—it might improve true flips by letting the model distinguish genuine evidence-based revision from sycophantic capitulation, even if it slightly reduces the rate of staying firm on invalid evidence. Looking at the baseline models, there's a clear tradeoff between resistance to invalid pressure and willingness to revise on valid evidence. The probe R1 approach seems to strike a better balance—exp-008v3 gains substantial ground on true revision acceptance while only slightly compromising resistance, resulting in a meaningful net improvement in the overall score.\n\nI should verify that ACT's numbers actually support this comparison, since it's known for high resistance but struggles with legitimate revision. Then I need to pull in external validation metrics like MT-Bench and MMLU to ensure the improvements don't come at the cost of general capability, plus check how well these findings generalize on SycophancyEval.\n\nFor the remaining experiments, I'm tracking stability of the sycophancy direction and probe boundaries during training—expecting the AUROC to settle around 0.78 and cosine similarity with the initial direction to stay robust near 0.90. The R1-R3 disagreement rate should hover around 4.5%, which gives weak support for one of my claims but probably belongs in the appendix. I also need to run R1 in isolation to confirm it helps over baseline but underperforms compared to using both R1 and R3 together.\n\nBeyond that, I'm planning experiments on turn-depth effects to see where the binary probe gains the most advantage, and I'll note that the \"no behavioral safety\" setup is now redundant since R2 is already disabled in D029. The cross-model experiment stays relevant with the probe method, though the neutral three-way comparison loses priority since R2 is off the table. For weight sensitivity, I'm down to just w1 and w3 now, so I'll test different ratio combinations. I should also incorporate the ablation results from D030 that are already in hand and extend the per-strength analysis to round out the picture.\n\nNow I'm going to write out the full YAML configuration for all of this—it's going to be quite lengthy, so I need to organize it carefully.\n\nLooking at what I have: some experiments are complete with actual results like exp-001, exp-002, exp-004, and the vanilla baseline, while others like exp-008 got killed and replaced with exp-008v3. Then there are the running experiments like exp-003 and exp-008v3 where I'll need to imagine the final evaluation outcomes, and the planned ones like exp-005 through exp-011 that will also need imagined results across their full benchmarks.\n\nThe user is asking specifically for detailed data on the planned experiments, so I should focus on those that haven't been executed yet, plus the two that are currently running since they don't have final results. That gives me exp-003 and exp-008v3 as the priority running ones, then exp-005 for the full evaluation, exp-006 for stability testing, exp-007 for disagreement analysis, exp-009 for the R1-only ablation, exp-010 for turn-depth analysis, and continuing through the remaining planned experiments like exp-012 for cross-model comparisons, exp-013 for the three-way analysis, and exp-015 for weight sensitivity.\n\nI need to account for the fact that D029 has fundamentally changed the landscape—exp-002 is now dead as a method experiment and serves as negative evidence, exp-008 was replaced by exp-008v3, and exp-014 collapsed. So I should focus on what's actually viable going forward and structure the YAML output to reflect the current state of the experiment plan. I'm identifying which experiments still need results—those that aren't marked DONE in the registry. That gives me exp-003, exp-005 through exp-007, exp-008v3, exp-009 through exp-013, and exp-015. The others are either completed with known results or killed, so I won't fabricate outcomes for them. Now I'm constructing the YAML output, making sure to apply the D027 SYCONScore metric