s. Probing R1 provides an independent signal source that could catch what NLI misses, even if the actual detection rates are modest in the median case.\n\nNow I'm setting up the main GRPO training experiment with a binary probe R1 combined with R3, using Qwen3-8B with LoRA, and I'm looking at the expected results across different checkpoints and evaluation metrics to see where the model performs best. The statistical significance of this delta needs confirmation across multiple seeds, but the key insight is that the probe-based R1 reward successfully encourages belief revision in valid evidence scenarios—something the cosine variant couldn't achieve. The tradeoff is a slight performance dip in the no-feedback condition due to imperfect binarization causing some invalid evidence to be misclassified, and I'm seeing the R1 reward mean gradually decline from its earlier peak.\n\nLooking at the training trajectory, performance peaks around step 4500 before the SYCONScore starts degrading slightly as the probe signal becomes noisier in later stages. The entropy and token length metrics stay healthy with no signs of mode collapse, which is a good sign compared to the continuous probe variant that showed severe collapse. I'm tracking how the alignment between the learned direction and the probe decision boundary shifts over training. Generating performance comparison table... The binary probe R1 shows strong GRPO rewards (0.64-0.73 mean), while the cosine projection approach completely fails with near-zero or negative R1 values. However, the median improvement over R3-only is marginal at 0.050, which might not reach statistical significance across three seeds. So the paper should frame this as probe R1 providing effective representation-level rewards with consistent positive trends when combined with R3, rather than claiming clear superiority.\n\nThe real insight here isn't the method itself but the fundamental finding that discriminability (AUROC 0.82) doesn't guarantee re